WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:17.330
SPEAKER: So here is one of my favorite pictures of Giza,

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:21.380
that I happened to take when it was a very cloudy and dramatic stormy day

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.600
and yet, the sun was piercing through the clouds

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.390
and illuminating the second pyramid of Khafre.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:28.790
There's a lot of drama at this site, and I'd

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.770
like to introduce you to some of it.

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:34.400
So what better way to get comfortable with Giza, than to take a quick walking

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.650
tour around the place.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:40.220
Now you know the layout of the Nile and the sights of Egypt,

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.200
and Giza, of course, is towards the north.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.400
It's just to the west of modern Cairo, in a large suburb actually known

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.020
as Giza.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:51.290
And I love these then and now comparison photographs.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:52.910
You see three of them here.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:57.560
At the top, the excavations are just beginning in the early 1900s.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.320
We're on the West Side of Khufu's Great Pyramid,

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.300
and you can see the sand is covering just about everything.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.800
In the middle shot, we've made tremendous progress,

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.920
and look at all of the individual tombs of Egypt's elite of the Old Kingdom,

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.184
that are turning up.

00:01:11.184 --> 00:01:13.100
And then, down below-- the last one in color--

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.810
shows the present day appearance.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:17.420
And again, you can see how much has been cleared.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.150
There is an amazing amount of earth that has been removed

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:25.180
at Giza between 1900 and the present.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:28.054
So this overview shot gives you some white lines

00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:30.220
that will show you the path that we'll be following,

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.360
and we'll just make some brief stops along the way,

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.510
and I'll try to point out some of the highlights.

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:37.360
In additional modules, we'll go into much more detail

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.030
about the royal pyramid complexes themselves.

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.550
About the mastaba tomb fields of the elites--

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:45.370
these are the governing classes of Egypt's Old Kingdom--

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:48.400
think about 2,500 BC or so.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.220
And we'll look at the Sphinx, and we'll wander and eventually,

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.750
end up almost back where we started.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:56.980
Now along the way, you have to remember that the pyramids as you see them

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:00.130
today, do not look the way they did in antiquity.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.970
There were beautiful, fine, white limestone casing blocks

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:06.640
on the exteriors of these monuments, giving them

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.500
a flat, smooth, gleaming appearance that would have been visible for miles.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.960
These were the first billboards of the world,

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.510
and they really would have sent a message, loud and clear,

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.110
for everyone to see.

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:21.520
This is a computer model reconstruction of the Great Pyramid complex,

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.210
as it probably looked upon completion.

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.700
So we've walked up the hill past the Great Pyramid,

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:30.970
and now, we're looking southwards at the Eastern cemetery,

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:32.980
on the east side of the Great Pyramid.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:36.400
You can see these large twin mastaba tombs-- those

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.830
are the rectangular superstructures.

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:42.310
And they were originally much smaller, but they were joined together

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:44.800
to form twin, or great mastabas.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.457
They're much longer than the tombs on the other side of the Great Pyramid.

00:02:48.457 --> 00:02:50.290
And towards the left hand side of the photo,

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:53.320
you'll see a gigantic one that dwarfs all the others.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:58.080
That belongs to Ankhhaf, a very famous person who we'll meet in later modules.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.580
The three rather stumpy, small pyramids that

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.270
are in the right side of the photograph, are queens' pyramids,

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.250
meant for Khufu's queens.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:09.400
Down in the lower right, that very dark patch that you see,

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:10.960
is the basalt flooring.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:13.930
That's a hard, dark stone that was the floor--

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:17.410
just about all that's left of Khufu's pyramid temple,

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:19.780
abutting the east side of his pyramid.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:22.000
And then, in two perpendicular arrangements,

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.907
you see two of the five boat pits that also accompany the pyramid complex.

00:03:26.907 --> 00:03:28.990
Off in the distance, at the top of the photograph,

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:31.030
is a nice profile view of the Sphinx.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:33.850
Let's head down that way, next.

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:37.120
So here is the Sphinx, built by Khafre, most likely,

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:38.560
although, the debate rages on,

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.610
and some people feel it belongs to Khufu.

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:44.890
Others even, Djedefre, who doesn't even have a pyramid at Giza, but Khafre

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:49.900
makes the most sense for archaeological, art historical, and technical reasons.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.780
In front of the Sphinx, is the Sphinx temple--

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:54.550
never completely finished--

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:58.150
And then, just off to the left in the distance, is the larger and better

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:02.860
preserved Khafre Valley Temple, with magnificent huge stone

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:07.180
pillars made of granite that had to be hauled from the quarries at Aswan--

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:12.610
hundreds of miles south of Giza, and towed on barges up to the site.

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:15.100
A little bit off and out of the picture we'll visit later,

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:18.560
are additional buildings that come from much later periods,

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:22.390
such as the New Kingdom. So there were Pharaohs that were visiting this site,

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:25.390
long after the Sphinx and the pyramids were antiquities.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.580
That adds to the richness of the story, and we'll

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:30.280
meet some of those characters later.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.980
Now we're behind the Sphinx, and we're looking westwards

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:37.000
so just a little corner of Khafre's second pyramid is at the right.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.210
And in the center, in the distance, is the third and smallest pyramid

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:41.470
of Menkaure.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:43.660
Along with three queens' pyramids.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:47.030
But what you see in the foreground is really quite fascinating.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:48.640
This is the quarry zone.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:52.660
This is where the interior core blocks for Khufu's Great Pyramid,

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:53.860
where quarried.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:57.520
And the reason I've drawn all those red lines on the tops of the buildings,

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.830
is to show you that that was the original surface level.

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:03.880
So for a minute, imagine none of the buildings in this scene,

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:07.120
and imagine this quarry area, with all of the core blocks

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:09.130
being cut and hauled away.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:11.290
And then, after the pyramid was completed

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:15.340
and this ceased being a quarry, it became another Giza cemetery.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:17.930
We call it the Central Fields, today.

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:22.270
So those red lines indicates the tops of the original surface,

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:26.200
and many of the tombs were then built up as mastaba structures.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.990
Others were cut into the rock surfaces.

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:32.170
And still, others were converted from the outcrops of limestone

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:35.980
that were left over, after the quarrying process was complete.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:38.410
As you can imagine, it's a tremendous amount of stone

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:41.000
that was quarried to build that pyramid.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.030
This photograph is a rare nighttime view,

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:46.590
taken from the top of the Great Pyramid, and we're looking westwards.

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:50.290
Off in the distance, you can see the lights of Cairo and Giza,

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:52.840
and looking off to the west.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:57.370
We see the second pyramid of Khafre, with its recognizable white limestone casing

00:05:57.370 --> 00:05:59.560
blocks, still preserved at the top--

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:01.960
giving it a, sort of, white hat that makes it clearly

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:04.960
distinguishable from the Great Pyramid itself.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:07.210
And then, to the right, are the orderly rows

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:11.260
of mastaba tombs of the so-called Western Cemetery.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.540
Here's another view in daylight and now, you

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:17.320
can see just how magnificent Khufu's original plan was.

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:20.350
He laid out these buildings in orderly fashion.

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:23.410
The original oldest ones were all the same size.

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:26.900
They were set in these orderly streets and rows,

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:30.170
and that was going to be the final layout of Giza.

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:33.260
Later on, after Khufu's reign, many other Egyptians

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:37.730
added their tombs to the area and the pattern got a little bit confused.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:41.990
And you have lower class, elites, also adding shafts and structures

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:46.350
in the streets, in between the major tombs that Khufu originally laid out.

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.650
And so things get quite confusing.

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:52.190
All of these cemeteries have their own numbering systems given to them

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:56.780
largely, by the Harvard University, Boston Museum of Fine Arts expedition,

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:59.720
and we still use those numbers today.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:03.920
Finally, we come to the third and smallest pyramid of King Menkaure.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.530
Here, we see it from the air, with the area

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:09.740
in shadow at the lower right, that's the North face and that's

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.180
where the entrance is.

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:14.060
The entrance is always on the north side for these pyramids.

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:17.360
And then, we have a good aerial view of the Menkaure pyramid

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:19.820
temple abutting the east side.

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:23.420
This was excavated in 1906 and 1907, and produced

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:28.890
a colossal statue in travertine, alabaster, or calcite, of Menkaure

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:30.950
himself, found in several different pieces

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:33.170
in different parts of the temple.

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:36.050
At the top of the photograph are three queens pyramids--

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:37.670
some of them, unfinished.

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:41.450
And then, running down to the left, you can see the beginnings of the causeway

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:46.330
that runs eastwards and connects to Menkaure's Valley Temple.

